# India's Rivers and Lakes: Himalayan and Peninsular Systems

> Two families of rivers drain India — young, snow-fed Himalayan
> streams and old, seasonal peninsular rivers. This lesson compares
> their behaviour and tours the country's major lakes.

## 1. The Himalayan Rivers: The Indus System

The **Indus** rises near Lake Manasarovar in Tibet, flows through
Ladakh, and leaves India for Pakistan before reaching the Arabian Sea.
Its Punjab tributaries — **Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas, and Sutlej** —
drain the world's greatest concentration of glaciers outside the poles.

## 2. The Himalayan Rivers: Ganga and Brahmaputra

The **Ganga** begins as the **Bhagirathi** from the Gangotri glacier,
joining the Alaknanda at Devprayag. Her great tributaries are the
**Yamuna**, **Ghaghara**, **Gandak**, and **Kosi** — the last so prone
to shifting course that it is called the **sorrow of Bihar**. Near the
Farakka barrage the river feeds the **Hooghly**, which carries her
waters to the Bay of Bengal.

The **Brahmaputra** is the **Tsangpo** in Tibet and the **Dihang** in
Arunachal Pradesh before becoming the Brahmaputra of Assam — where
summer flooding earns it the name **sorrow of Assam**. Midway lies
**Majuli**, one of the world's largest inhabited river islands.

[note]
Snowmelt plus monsoon rain gives Himalayan rivers their perennial,
ever-flowing character — navigable in stretches and generous with
irrigation and hydropower.
[/note]

## 3. Peninsular Rivers

| East-flowing | West-flowing |
| --- | --- |
| Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri | Narmada, Tapi, and the short Western Ghats streams |
| Rise in the Western Ghats / Chotanagpur, drain to the Bay of Bengal | Rise in central India, drain to the Arabian Sea |
| End in large deltas | End in estuaries — no deltas |
| Rain-fed, seasonal | Rain-fed, seasonal |

The **Godavari**, largest of the peninsular rivers, is honoured as the
**Dakshin Ganga**. The **Narmada** and **Tapi** flow through **rift
valleys** — fault troughs so steep and tide-scoured that sediment
cannot build deltas, only estuaries.

## 4. Why the Two Systems Behave Differently

| Feature | Himalayan rivers | Peninsular rivers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Water regime | Perennial — rain plus snowmelt | Seasonal — rain only |
| Age and gradient | Young, steep, energetic | Ancient, graded, gentle |
| Channel habit | Deep gorges, sweeping meanders | Straighter, steadier courses on hard rock |
| Mouth | Great deltas (Sundarbans) | East: deltas; west: estuaries |
| Flood risk | Widespread summer floods | Mostly flash floods |

[example]
The Kosi is a textbook case of a restless young river: leaving the
mountains, it spreads an alluvial fan across north Bihar and wanders
over it, century by century.
[/example]

## 5. India's Major Lakes

@diagram tree id="indian-rivers-by-coast" layout="tree" aria="Indian rivers grouped by system and coast"
  "Indian rivers" -> "Himalayan system"
  "Indian rivers" -> "Peninsular system"
  "Himalayan system" -> "Indus, Ganga, Brahmaputra"
  "Peninsular system" -> "East-flowing to deltas"
  "Peninsular system" -> "West-flowing to estuaries"
@/diagram

| Lake | State | Distinction |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Wular | Jammu & Kashmir | Largest freshwater lake (fed by the Jhelum) |
| Dal | Srinagar | Houseboats and shikaras; tourist icon |
| Sambhar | Rajasthan | Largest inland salt lake |
| Chilika | Odisha | Largest brackish-water lagoon |
| Vembanad | Kerala | Longest lake; heart of the backwaters |
| Lonar | Maharashtra | Crater lake of meteoric origin |

[definition]
A **lake** is any inland body of standing water; a **lagoon** (like
Chilika) is a shallow lake cut off from the sea by a sand bar; an
**estuary** (like the Hooghly mouth) is a drowned river valley where
tide and stream meet.
[/definition]

## 6. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Sorrow of Bihar? | The Kosi |
| Sorrow of Assam? | The Brahmaputra |
| Dakshin Ganga? | The Godavari |
| Rift-valley rivers? | Narmada and Tapi |
| Largest freshwater lake? | Wular |
| Largest brackish lagoon? | Chilika |
| Longest lake of India? | Vembanad |
